# Supabase. Both are public: the anon key is meant to reach the browser, and
# every query it makes is subject to RLS.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL is the Project URL from Supabase settings — a bare
# host, nothing else. Not the dashboard link you get from the address bar, which
# looks like https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/<ref> and fails CORS.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# On a host: NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are inlined into the browser bundle when the
# app is BUILT. Adding them without triggering a new deployment leaves the
# browser with nothing, while the server sees them — so sign-in fails while
# everything looks configured. Set them, then redeploy.

# There is deliberately no service-role key here. It belongs only to the display
# bundle route and its server-only siblings, and never to the dashboard.

# Same reason there is no SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET here. It signs the short-lived
# Realtime authorization token minted by /api/screen/[token]/realtime-auth —
# Project Settings → API → JWT Settings → JWT Secret — and never reaches the
# browser.

# Same reason there is no CRON_SECRET here either. It's the bearer token
# /api/cron/build-bundles (and /api/cron/warm-zmanim) checks before running.

# There is no ZMANIM_CHABAD_ENABLED here either — not a secret, but a
# deliberate decision (plan.md §10.4: no ToS conversation with Chabad.org
# has happened yet), not a default to ship in a committed file. Set to the
# exact string "true" to offer Chabad.org as a zmanim source; anything else,
# including unset, keeps it off everywhere (docs/environment.md).

# There is no GEOCODING_API_KEY here either. It's the LocationIQ access token
# behind the address lookup on the shul settings and new-shul forms — a
# metered credential, so it stays server-side rather than shipping in the
# browser bundle for anyone to spend. Without it both forms say so and fall
# back to manual latitude/longitude, which always works (docs/environment.md).
